The problem is of type quadratic in the auxiliary implementation $y = x^3+kx+4$, so we can find $k$ by setting it equal to zero and solving for $k$.

\begin{align*}
y &= x^3 + kx + 4 \\
0 &= y - 4 \\
0 &= x^3 + kx - 4
\end{align*}

To find the real roots of $x^3 + kx - 4$, we can use the Rational Root Theorem, which states that possible rational roots are factors of the constant term (in this case, $\pm 1$, $\pm 2$, or $\pm 4$).

Test the numerical values of the rational roots as factors:

\begin{align*}
\text{If } x = 1 \colon \begin{cases} 1^3 + k(1) - 4 = -k + 3 \\ k \approx 6 \end{cases} \\
\text{If } x = 2 \colon \begin{cases} 2^3 + k(2) - 4 = 8 + 2k - 4 = 2k \\ k \approx 2 \text{ or } k \approx -6 \end{cases} \\
\text{If } x = 4 \colon \begin{cases} 4^3 + k(4) - 4 = 64 + 4k - 4 = 4k \\ k \approx 16 \text{ or } k \approx -16 \end{cases}
\end{align*}

Since coefficients of a polynomial determines the nature of its roots, we know that neither $k \approx -6$ nor $k \approx -16$ can be a solution for positive real $k$. This means that $k$ must be either $k \approx 2$ or $k \approx 6$.

Let us test for two cases $k = 6$ and $k = 2$.

\begin{align*}
k = 6 \colon x^4 + 6x^3 + x^2 + 24x + 16 \quad \text{By factoring out $x^2$, we find:} \quad (x^2 + 6x + 1)(x^2 + 4) \\
x^2 + 6x + 1 = (x+1)^2(x+5) \quad \text{So we have:} \quad (x+1)^2(x+5)(x^2 + 4) = 0 \\
\text{Each factor gives a different real number, so there are four distinct real roots:} x = -1, -1 \pm\sqrt{5}, -2.
\end{align*}

\begin{align*}
k = 2 \colon x^4 + 2x^3 + x^2 + 8x + 16 \quad \text{By factoring out $(x + 4)$, we find:} \quad (x^3 + 4)(x + 4) + x^2 \quad \\
\text{Now we can perform long division,} \quad x^3 + 4 = (x^2 + 5) - (x+1)^2 \\
\text{So,} \quad x^4 + 2x^3 + x^2 + 8x + 16 = (x^2 + 5)(x+4) +(x+1)^2 \\
\text{Continuing long division,} \quad x+1 = \frac{(x^2 + 5) - (x + 4) - x - 4}{-6} \\
\text{What we obtain is a remainder, and it is easy to verify $x+1 = -\frac{6}{x+5}$, so we have} \\
\text{When } x \neq -5 \colon (x+1)^2 = -\frac{36}{(x+5)^2}
\end{align*}

Now, we can see that the left side of the equation
\begin{align*}
x^4 + 2x^3 + x^2 + 8x + 16 &= (x^2 + 5)(x+4) + (x+1)^2 \\
&= (x^2 + 5)(x+4) + \left( -\frac{36}{(x+5)^2} \right)
\end{align*}
is a decreasing function for $x > -5$, while the right side is an increasing function. Since these two functions intersect in the interval $(-5, -\frac{36}{16} - 4)$ and are not continuous from the left at $x=-5$, there will be only one real root.

\boxed{k \approx 6}

