The MidPoint function calculates the midpoint of two points: y coordinates and x coordinates $(x_1, x_2)$ if the extra parameter $y$ is provided, else calculates slope ($\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}$) and go forward-backward calculation:
```alas
void MidPoint(now: UnitVector2D $p$,
                int $mode$ := 0
               )
{
    if (mode = 2)
    {
        Real y = p.y_2();
        (p.x_2(), p.y_2()) = MidPoint((p.x(), p.y()), y);
        return p;
    }

    Real m = PerpendicularSlope(slope($p$));
    Real x_mid = ($p.x_2() + $p.x_1()) / 2.;
    m *= ($p.y_2() - $p.y_1()) / ($p.x_2() - $p.x_1) - m;
    Real y_mid = $p.y_1() + m * (x_mid - p.x_1());
    return Point2D(x_mid, y_mid);
}
```

In this MidPoint function implementation, $mode$ can be used to indicate if the provided point has already been determined and only the midpoint (2) or both the original and midpoint need to be returned (0). In the function, compare using the PerpendicularSlope method with the slope of $p$ in order to keep the Euclidean distance equal to the original segment while leveraging the perpendicular directions ($\bot$) for numerical stability.

