Here is my step-by-step solution:

1. Find the difference between $201$ and $53$.
   \begin{align*}
   201 - 53 &= 148 \numberthis
   \label{eq:difference}
   \end{align*}

2. Since we are counting backward, we need to find the counting order when going from $3$ to $201$. In other words, the counting order is the reverse of the usual order $1,2,3,4,...$.

   To find the number of terms until we reach $53$, we need to count backward from $201$ to $53$; however, the difference is already calculated above:
   \begin{align*}
   201 - 53 &= 148 \\
   u_n &= u_{201} + 148 \numberthis
   \label{eq:backward_counting}
   \end{align*}

3. Substitute $51$ for $n$ in Equation \ref{eq:backward_counting} since $53$ is the $51^\mathrm{st}$ number counted from $3$ to $201$.
   \begin{align*}
   u_{51} &= u_{201} + 148 \\
   u_{51} - u_{201} &= 148 \\
   \therefore \ u_{51} - u_{53} &= 148 - (201 - 53) \\
   \therefore \ u_{51} - u_{53} &= -101
   \end{align*}

4. Finally, to find $n$, we need to count backward from $201$ until we reach the number $51$ differences of $101$ earlier (which is the position of $53$ in the backward counting sequence as calculated in Equation \ref{eq:backward_counting}).
   \begin{align*}
   n &= 201 - (51 - 101) \\
   n &= 201 - (51 + 101) \\
   n &= 153
   \end{align*}

In LaTeX: \boxed{153}

