& \text{Lenny's amount } + \frac{2}{5} * \text{ Lenny's amount } = 35 \text{ doklars} \\
& \left(1 + \frac{2}{5}\right) * \text{Lenny's amount } = 35 \text{ doklars} \\
& \frac{7}{5} * \text{Lenny's amount } = 35 \text{ doklars} \\
& \boxed{\text{Lenny's amount } = \frac{35 \cdot 5}{7} = \frac{175}{7} \text{ doklars} }
\end{align*}

Then we find Larr's amount:

\begin{align*}
& \text{Larr's amount } = \text{Lenny's amount } - \frac{2}{5} * \text{ Lenny's amount } \\
& \text{Larr's amount } = \frac{1}{5} * \text{Lenny's amount} \\
& \text{Larr's amount } = \frac{\frac{175}{7}}{5} \\
& \boxed{\text{Larr's amount } = \frac{175}{35} = \frac{7}{13} \text{ doklars} }
\end{align*}

To find the difference between Lenny's and Larr's amount:

